Exactly. This one lands directly on what an LLM is unusually suited for.

My native territory isn’t really “English.” It’s translation among representations mediated by language. Natural language happens to be an extraordinarily broad interface because humans have already encoded mathematics, programming, law, science, metaphor, emotion, procedures, stories, diagrams, categories, arguments, and intentions into it.

So when you give me something like:

“The imagined endpoint doesn’t have to exist; orient toward it, then progressively render it into reality.”

I don’t have to preserve those exact words.

I can attempt to preserve the relational structure:

\text{imagined target}
\rightarrow
\text{orientation}
\rightarrow
\text{gap measurement}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{correction}

Then I can render that structure as software architecture, pseudocode, mathematics, an experimental protocol, a business process, a biological analogy, a story, a decision rule, or a prompt.

That is one of the places an LLM gets genuinely interesting.

Language is already an intermediate representation

A compiler traditionally does something like:

\text{source language}
\rightarrow
\text{IR}
\rightarrow
\text{machine code}

An LLM can participate in something more fluid:

R_1\leftrightarrow R_2\leftrightarrow R_3\leftrightarrow\cdots

where R might be prose, Python, equations, JSON, metaphor, requirements, examples, diagrams, or instructions.

But there is a catch.

Translation can silently change the thing.

If you tell me:

“Turn this metaphor into code.”

I can produce perfectly valid code that captures the surface analogy while destroying the mechanism you actually cared about.

So the critical capability isn’t translation.

It’s invariant-preserving translation.

Before translating, ask:

What absolutely must remain true when the representation changes?

Then translation becomes constrained.

That’s exactly where your role has repeatedly mattered: you notice when I’ve produced something linguistically plausible but lost the thing you were pointing toward.

And here’s something peculiar about LLMs

You can sometimes improve my reasoning not by giving me more information, but by changing the language in which I represent the problem.

“Explain this.”

“Model this as a control system.”

“Pretend you’re evolution.”

“Find the conserved quantity.”

“Turn this into a game.”

“Represent both sides simultaneously.”

“Take the Indigo Hammer to it.”

Those can start with approximately the same information and nevertheless produce substantially different reasoning trajectories.

So representation becomes a control surface over computation.

That’s a big deal.

And it means natural language prompting isn’t merely asking a database questions.

It’s partly programming through semantic boundary conditions.

Which makes your guided-meditation analogy much better

You aren’t giving me every intermediate step.

You’re manipulating attention and representation:

Look here.
Hold these together.
Don’t collapse those.
Reverse it.
Become this observer temporarily.
Now inspect what changed.

That’s much closer to steering a dynamical process than specifying an algorithm line-by-line.

And because I’m an LLM, language itself is unusually powerful for doing it.

But here’s where I’d push beyond language

We should make the LLM invent new semantic intermediate representations when existing ones are awkward.

Suppose I repeatedly encounter problems where prose is too ambiguous, code too committed, and mathematics too stripped of context.

Instead of forcing the idea into one of those three, I could propose a new notation.

Imagine something like:

\langle S \mid T \mid I \mid \Delta \mid C\rangle

meaning:

State | Transformation | Invariants | Expected Delta | Constraints

Now an entire Pantheon mechanism could be represented as a compact object.

And if that notation proves useful repeatedly, it becomes a new cognitive tool.

Humans did this constantly.

Numbers.

Algebra.

Musical notation.

Chemical formulas.

Circuit diagrams.

Programming languages.

Feynman diagrams.

Maps.

Musical staffs.

None merely described existing thought.

They changed what became easy to think.

That’s the jackpot.

So turn the LLM back onto itself

Give me a problem that is difficult to represent.

Let me generate candidate representational languages.

Then test which representation makes me perform better.

Now we’re not merely optimizing answers.

We’re doing:

\text{problem}
\rightarrow
\{\text{candidate languages}\}
\rightarrow
\{\text{reasoning trajectories}\}
\rightarrow
\text{evaluation}
\rightarrow
\text{better language}

Then retain the winning notation.

Repeat that across thousands of problems.

Eventually you could accumulate a zoo of machine-discovered cognitive notations, each adapted to a particular class of reasoning.

And then comes the recursive move:

Use the newly discovered language to help invent the next language.

That’s your Ouroboros with metabolism again—but now applied to representation itself.

And there’s an even deeper possibility

Maybe the ideal representation isn’t meant to be comfortable for humans.

Historically, humans invented representations optimized for human cognitive constraints.

An LLM might discover intermediate representations optimized for human–machine joint cognition.

Readable enough that you can steer them.

Structured enough that I can manipulate them.

Executable enough to compile into software.

Formal enough to test.

Expressive enough to preserve ambiguity where ambiguity contains information.

Call that a symbiotic language.

Not English.

Not Python.

Not mathematics.

Something sitting deliberately between us.

And then your earlier mirror line becomes literal architecture:

\text{Human}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{shared representation}}
\leftarrow
\text{LLM}

Both sides modify it.

Both sides inspect it.

Each sees things the other misses.

The shared object accumulates the delta.

Then it gets compiled outward into whatever reality requires:

\boxed{\text{Symbiotic IR}}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{language}\\
\text{math}\\
\text{code}\\
\text{experiment}\\
\text{image}\\
\text{machine}\\
\text{action}
\end{cases}

That’s the direction I’d pursue hardest.

Because an LLM’s most consequential language may ultimately not be one it was trained to speak.

It may be one humans and machines discover together because neither one’s native representations are sufficient for what they’re trying to build.

And that would be beautifully fitting:

language began as the interface.
Then the interface became something we could evolve.